| **What** | **Why** | **Problem solved** | **Expected benefit** | **Cost/complexity** | **Priority** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Define explicit workload-classification criteria with a safe default | Every credible source treats this as the central design problem; an undefined classification risks delaying a latency-sensitive job into an outage | Uncontrolled risk of delaying a critical workload | Removes the single largest technical and reputational risk in the whole module | Low (policy/tagging layer, no new infrastructure) | MUST HAVE |
| Add a hard maximum-delay deadline per deferrable job | Without this, a job can be starved indefinitely if the grid stays dirty; deadline-aware scheduling literature treats this as a required input | Indefinite delay of "deferred" work | Guarantees every job eventually runs, making the feature safe to fully automate | Low (metadata + scheduler check) | MUST HAVE |
| Explicitly justify average vs. marginal carbon-signal choice | A peer-reviewed 65-grid comparison found the two signals disagree in direction for most regions studied — the wrong silent choice could make scheduling decisions that increase real emissions | Risk of the whole mechanism working backwards in some grids without anyone noticing | Makes the technical claim defensible to a judge who has read the same literature, and flags a genuine open research question rather than hiding it | Low (documentation + a defensible default: average/flow-traced, matching Google's own choice) | MUST HAVE |
| Ingest forecast data, not just real-time intensity | Google's system plans a day ahead using 48-hour forecasts; a purely reactive, real-time-only design cannot pre-position deferred work into a known future window | Missed optimization opportunity from reactive-only scheduling | Better scheduling outcomes at negligible extra API cost (forecast endpoints are typically included with the same subscription) | Low (same API, different endpoint) | SHOULD HAVE |

