| **What** | **Why** | **Problem solved** | **Expected benefit** | **Cost/complexity** | **Priority** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Hysteresis / smoothing on the carbon-state signal | A receding-horizon study found forecast noise increases scheduling volatility even when it does not reduce total savings; smoothing prevents unnecessary job thrashing | Excess job start/stop churn from reacting to every data-point fluctuation | More stable, predictable scheduling behaviour with no loss of achieved carbon savings | Low (simple moving-average/threshold band) | SHOULD HAVE |
| Cross-wire with PowerPrune's capacity state | Prevents GridSync from scheduling deferred jobs into a window where the servers needed to run them have been powered down | Silent scheduling failure from a compute-capacity blind spot | Demonstrates VerdeGrid's actual differentiator — coordinated modules, not five silos | Low-Medium (integration only, both modules already track this data internally) | MUST HAVE |
| Electricity-price signal alongside carbon | Carbon and price are not always correlated; useful secondary optimization for a cost-sensitive operator | Occasional scheduling into an expensive-but-green window when a similarly-green cheaper window exists nearby | Adds a second, tangible cost-savings number alongside CO2 avoided | Low-Medium (second API integration, e.g., day-ahead price feeds where available) | SHOULD HAVE |

